<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jeanne Mance</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1606">1606</a>–<a href="/years/1673">1673</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jeanne Mance was a French nurse and settler who co-founded Ville-Marie, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-de-chomedey-de-maisonneuve">Paul de Chomedey de Maisonneuve</a> in 1642. She founded and ran the Hôtel-Dieu de Montréal, the city's first hospital, and helped to secure the funds and settlers that saved the struggling colony. She is honoured as a co-founder of Montreal.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jeanne-mance.jpg" alt="Jeanne Mance" width="320" height="512" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sculpture : Louis-Philippe Hébert / Photo : Jeangagnon · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JeanneManceHotelDieu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jeanne Mance was born on 12 November 1606 in the cathedral city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langres">Langres</a> in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the second of twelve children of Charles Mance, a royal prosecutor, and Catherine Emonnot, whose family also worked in the law. After her father died in 1630 and her mother in 1632, she and her elder sister Marguerite supported their younger siblings. She chose neither marriage nor a religious order, and in the 1630s, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> and outbreaks of plague struck Langres, she learned her skills by tending the sick and wounded.</p><p>At 34, on a pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troyes">Troyes</a>, she resolved to go to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a>, supported by Anne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a>. Charles Lallemant recruited her for the Société Notre-Dame de Montréal, which aimed to convert the native peoples and found a hospital in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a> like the one at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Mance sailed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-rochelle">La Rochelle</a> on 9 May 1641. After a winter at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> she reached the Island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a> with Paul Chomedey de Maisonneuve in the spring of 1642 and began caring for the sick in her own home. In 1645, with a gift of 6,000 francs from Angélique Bullion, she opened the hospital on Rue Saint-Paul, which she directed for 17 years.</p><p>On a journey to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1650 she obtained 22,000 livres from the Duchesse d'Aiguillon. Finding the colony threatened by Iroquois attacks on her return, she lent hospital money to Maisonneuve, who went to France to raise a hundred men for its defence. A second journey in 1657 brought three Hospital Sisters of St. Joseph from La Fleche, Judith Moreau de Bresoles, Catherine Mace and Marie Maillet, who reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a> in October 1659 after a passage struck by plague.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Mance died in 1673 and was buried in the hospital's church, which was demolished with her house in 1696; the Religious Hospitallers of St. Joseph carried on her work, and the hospital moved to the foot of Mount Royal in 1861. In 1946 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> added her to the Founders of the Canadian Church, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-francis">Pope Francis</a> declared her Venerable on 7 November 2014. Rue Jeanne-Mance and Jeanne-Mance Park in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a> bear her name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jeanne Mance” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Trois-Rivières was founded in 1634 and Ville-Marie, later Montreal, in 1642 by Paul de Chomedey de Maisonneuve and <b>Jeanne Mance</b> as a religious mission.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-france">New France · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langres">Langres</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/canada">Canada</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.86,5.33&amp;year=1649" aria-label="Langres on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4674 487 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jeanne Mance"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5124 757h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5124 757h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.86,5.33&amp;year=1649">What was here in 1649? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
